      content: '**Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Selection**:\n\n1. **First-line: Levetiracetam (Keppra)**:\n   - Restart at 500 mg BID, titrate to 750-1000 mg BID over 1-2 weeks\n   - **Advantages in TBI**: No hepatic enzyme induction (important for drug interactions), no effect on bone density, weight-neutral, rapid titration, IV formulation available, does not impair cognitive recovery\n   - **Disadvantages**: Psychiatric side effects — irritability, depression, behavioral changes in up to 10-15% of patients. Given her pre-existing anxiety and the cognitive effects of TBI, monitor closely\n   - If psychiatric side effects develop, consider adding pyridoxine (vitamin B6) 50 mg daily (some evidence for mitigating levetiracetam-related irritability) or switch agents\n\n2. **Alternative agents**:\n   - **Lamotrigine**: Excellent cognitive profile, mood-stabilizing properties. Limitation is very slow titration (8 weeks to therapeutic dose) — not ideal acutely\n   - **Lacosamide**: Good tolerability, minimal cognitive effects, can be titrated faster than lamotrigine\n   - **Oxcarbazepine**: Effective for focal epilepsy but risk of hyponatremia\n\n3. **Agents to AVOID in TBI rehabilitation**:\n   - **Phenytoin**: Impairs cognitive recovery, complex pharmacokinetics, induces hepatic enzymes, causes cerebellar toxicity\n   - **Phenobarbital**: Severe cognitive sedation, impairs neuroplasticity\n   - **Topiramate**: Significant cognitive side effects ("Dopamax"), word-finding difficulty, would worsen her existing deficits\n   - **Benzodiazepines**: Impair memory, cause sedation, tolerance develops\n\n**Driving Restrictions**:\n- **Most states require a seizure-free interval** before resuming driving, typically 3-12 months depending on the state (most commonly 6 months)\n- Physician has an **obligation to inform the patient** of driving restrictions\n- Some states require **mandatory physician reporting** to the DMV (California, Delaware, Nevada, New Jersey, Oregon, Pennsylvania); others rely on self-reporting by the patient\n- This patient must be informed today that she **cannot drive** until she meets the seizure-free interval required by her state\n- Given she is a single mother who drives her children — transportation planning is critical for discharge\n\n**Rehabilitation Program Modifications**:\n1. **Fall precautions**: Upgrade to high fall risk. No unassisted balance activities, no elevated surfaces (balance beam contraindicated), no aquatic therapy unsupervised\n2. **Seizure precautions**: All therapy staff trained in seizure first aid. Keep suction and oxygen at bedside. Time seizures and document semiology.\n3. **Therapy scheduling**: Avoid scheduling high-risk activities when seizures are most likely (often early morning or with fatigue/sleep deprivation)\n4. **Helmet use**: If craniectomy site is present or skull integrity is compromised, helmet use is mandatory. In this patient with intact skull, a helmet is recommended during balance/gait training until seizure control is established\n5. **Supervision level**: Increase to contact-guard assist minimum for all mobility activities\n6. **AED blood level monitoring**: Levetiracetam levels (therapeutic range 12-46 mcg/mL) to ensure compliance and adequate dosing',
